{
  "id": "17-live-intentionally",
  "title": "Live Intentionally",
  "tenet": "Notice, choose, and bless",
  "durationMs": 94032,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/17-live-intentionally.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/17-live-intentionally.json",
  "text": "What might change if you noticed a lure before it became a choice?\n\nA young woman reached for her phone whenever her work paused. One afternoon a message arrived with a cruel story about a neighbor. Her thumb was already moving to forward it when she stopped. She noticed the small thrill of being first with the news, and the unease beneath it. She set the phone down and asked whether sharing would help anyone. Then she sent the neighbor a different message: Are you all right? That small pause did not make her perfect. It gave love time to speak before an impulse became an action. Later, when she spoke carelessly herself, she apologized and repaired what she could.\n\nPaul calls us to walk by the Holy Spirit rather than follow every impulse. To become the new person in Christ is to practice that choice in ordinary moments. Notice the lure early: loneliness, pride, an endless scroll, or gossip disguised as concern. Name it. Ask what love requires. If you have gone too far, apologize, confess, and begin again. Choose what you hear and watch with care, because repeated attention can shape learned desires. Listen to God's Word, grow a skill that helps someone you care about, or act on a good desire tested by Scripture and love. Intentional rest is good too; not every quiet minute must become work. Ask each morning whom you might bless. At night, remember one moment when you followed the Spirit rather than the impulse. Christ is the new Adam; in him, your next faithful choice can bear fruit.",
